  References
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[1] &lt;a href="https://headlamp.dev/docs/latest/installation/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Headlamp installation and authentication&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[2] &lt;a href="https://headlamp.dev/docs/latest/installation/in-cluster/dex/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Headlamp OAuth2 Proxy and Dex guide&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[3] &lt;a href="https://headlamp.dev/docs/latest/installation/in-cluster/identity-aware-proxy/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Headlamp identity-aware proxy guide&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[4] &lt;a href="https://www.zoomeye.ai/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ZoomEye&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally published on the &lt;a href="https://www.sharpquest.com/blog/posts/2024/march/how-to-migrate-mobile-apps-from-xamarin-and-xamarin-forms-to-net-maui/?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;amp;utm_campaign=blog-xamarin-maui" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;SharpQuest blog&lt;/a&gt;, written by Greg Kabacinski and Rutul Mehta from our .NET MAUI team. First published in March 2024 and updated for .NET 10, current tooling and the 2026 app store requirements.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Key takeaways&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Microsoft ended support for Xamarin on May 1, 2024. Xamarin's last versions no longer meet Google Play and App Store requirements, so Xamarin apps can't ship updates.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;.NET MAUI is the successor to Xamarin.Forms: one project and one codebase for Android, iOS, macOS and Windows. Migrate to .NET 10 (MAUI 10), the current long-term support release.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Before migrating, update the app to Xamarin.Forms 5 and current dependencies.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;There are two ways to migrate: a migration tool, or a new .NET MAUI project with your code copied in. We combine both. The .NET Upgrade Assistant is deprecated; the GitHub Copilot modernization agent in Visual Studio replaces it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Plan time for layouts, custom renderers and third-party packages. These take the most effort.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;.NET Multi-platform App UI (.NET MAUI) is the successor to Xamarin.Forms and Microsoft's framework for cross-platform apps with .NET. It lets you write code once and run it on Android, iOS, macOS and Windows. .NET MAUI is based on Xamarin.Forms, but it brings many improvements:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A single project instead of one project per platform.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A unified app model for creating and configuring apps across platforms.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A modernized control library built on native controls, with support for customization.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A new handler architecture that improves performance and reduces app size.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Community toolkits with additional controls, behaviors and utilities.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why Xamarin apps need to move
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Microsoft ended support for all Xamarin SDKs, including Xamarin.Forms, on May 1, 2024. There are no more bug fixes, security updates or new features. The last Xamarin releases target Android API 34 and Xcode 15.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That now blocks releases. Since April 28, 2026, Apple requires App Store uploads to be built with Xcode 26, and since August 31, 2026, Google Play requires new apps and updates to target Android 16 (API 36). A Xamarin app already in the stores keeps running for now, but you can't publish an update to it, not even a security fix.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you're still on Xamarin, plan the migration to .NET MAUI now. The rest of this guide covers the steps, and the problems you're likely to hit along the way. (If you're still deciding whether to migrate or rewrite, see &lt;a href="https://www.sharpquest.com/blog/posts/2026/october/still-on-xamarin-in-2026/?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;amp;utm_campaign=blog-xamarin-maui" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Still on Xamarin in 2026?&lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Migration benefits
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Current .NET and C#.&lt;/strong&gt; You get the latest C# language features, .NET Hot Reload and the performance work in each .NET release. Target .NET 10 today; see &lt;a href="https://devblogs.microsoft.com/dotnet/performance-improvements-in-net-10/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Performance improvements in .NET 10&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;One project, one codebase&lt;/strong&gt; for all platforms, which simplifies development and maintenance.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Native controls and handlers&lt;/strong&gt; you can customize, plus the community toolkits.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A future-proof app&lt;/strong&gt; that runs on the latest versions of Android and iOS, with ongoing updates from Microsoft and the .NET community.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Stay current after you migrate, too. Each major version of .NET MAUI is supported for 6 months after the next one ships: MAUI 8 ended in May 2025 and MAUI 9 in May 2026. Plan a yearly upgrade, just as you would for any other .NET app.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Prerequisites
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Visual Studio 2026&lt;/strong&gt; (or Visual Studio 2022 version 17.14 or later) with the &lt;strong&gt;.NET Multi-platform App UI development&lt;/strong&gt; workload. On macOS, use Visual Studio Code with the .NET MAUI extension; Visual Studio for Mac has been retired.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A Mac with Xcode 26&lt;/strong&gt; to build and sign the iOS app; the App Store requires it since April 28, 2026.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;.NET 10 SDK.&lt;/strong&gt; Target .NET 10 (MAUI 10). .NET 8 and .NET 9 reach end of support on November 10, 2026.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Xamarin.Forms 5.0&lt;/strong&gt; in your app. Xamarin.Forms 4.8 is the minimum, but upgrading to 5.0 first removes many differences before you start. Shared code should target .NET Standard 2.0 or later.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Up-to-date dependencies.&lt;/strong&gt; Update your NuGet packages, especially those that have .NET-compatible versions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Migration options
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. Tool-assisted upgrade.&lt;/strong&gt; The .NET Upgrade Assistant is now deprecated. Microsoft's replacement is the &lt;a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/porting/github-copilot-app-modernization/faq" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub Copilot modernization agent&lt;/a&gt;, included in Visual Studio 2026 (and Visual Studio 2022 17.14.17 and later). It analyzes your projects and dependencies, proposes a plan, applies code changes and commits each step so you can review it or roll it back. Treat its output as a first pass: review every change, and expect to finish layouts, renderers and packages by hand.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. New .NET MAUI project.&lt;/strong&gt; Create a new .NET MAUI project with the same name as your Xamarin.Forms app, then copy in your code files, XAML files, images, fonts and other assets, and update the code to the .NET MAUI APIs. This gives you the most control and a clean single-project structure, but it takes more effort. Microsoft documents the process in &lt;a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/maui/migration/multi-project-to-multi-project" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Manually upgrade a Xamarin.Forms app&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Our approach combines both.&lt;/strong&gt; We run the tool first to get the project files, namespaces and package references converted, then create a new single-project MAUI app and move the code across, using the converted project as a reference. We've moved a &lt;a href="https://www.sharpquest.com/portfolio/home-care-and-hospice-patient-caregiver-app/?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;amp;utm_campaign=blog-xamarin-maui" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;home-care patient app&lt;/a&gt; and an &lt;a href="https://www.sharpquest.com/portfolio/employee-time-tracking-mobile-app/?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;amp;utm_campaign=blog-xamarin-maui" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;employee time-tracking app&lt;/a&gt; from Xamarin to .NET MAUI this way.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Migration steps
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. Back up and branch.&lt;/strong&gt; Make sure the Xamarin.Forms app is in source control (such as Git) so you can return to a working state at any point.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Update to Xamarin.Forms 5.&lt;/strong&gt; Update the app and its dependencies to the latest versions and make sure it still runs. This keeps the API differences with .NET MAUI small and moves you onto .NET-compatible package versions where they exist.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. Convert the project.&lt;/strong&gt; Use the tool-assisted upgrade, a new MAUI project, or both, as described above.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;4. Fix namespaces.&lt;/strong&gt; Compare the files of the Xamarin.Forms app with the converted MAUI project and make sure the namespaces match Microsoft's migration guidance.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;5. Move startup code to &lt;code&gt;MauiProgram&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/strong&gt; .NET MAUI has a platform-independent startup class, &lt;code&gt;MauiProgram&lt;/code&gt;, as the app's entry point. If you've used ASP.NET Core, it will look familiar: an app builder where you register services in the dependency injection container and configure the app. Packages built for MAUI are usually set up in &lt;code&gt;MauiProgram&lt;/code&gt; rather than in the platform startup classes (&lt;code&gt;MainApplication&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;MainActivity&lt;/code&gt; on Android, &lt;code&gt;AppDelegate&lt;/code&gt; on iOS). Some configuration, such as push notifications, still belongs in those classes. &lt;code&gt;MainActivity&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;AppDelegate&lt;/code&gt; also look quite different from Xamarin's, so compare yours with a new project created from the MAUI template.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;6. Update APIs.&lt;/strong&gt; Most of Xamarin.Essentials is now built into .NET MAUI (see &lt;a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/maui/platform-integration/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;platform integration&lt;/a&gt;) and is included automatically with &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;UseMaui&amp;gt;true&amp;lt;/UseMaui&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; in your project file. To find where an API moved, use the &lt;a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/?view=net-maui-10.0" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;.NET API browser&lt;/a&gt;. The Xamarin Community Toolkit is now &lt;code&gt;CommunityToolkit.Maui&lt;/code&gt;, with many of the same features. If you use &lt;code&gt;MessagingCenter&lt;/code&gt;, replace it with &lt;code&gt;WeakReferenceMessenger&lt;/code&gt; from &lt;code&gt;CommunityToolkit.Mvvm&lt;/code&gt;. For lists, &lt;code&gt;CollectionView&lt;/code&gt; is the recommended replacement for &lt;code&gt;ListView&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;7. Fix layouts.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;RelativeLayout&lt;/code&gt; is removed. Replace it with a &lt;code&gt;Grid&lt;/code&gt; or another layout. (The compatibility namespace still lets you use it, but we don't recommend it.)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The &lt;code&gt;...AndExpand&lt;/code&gt; options on &lt;code&gt;StackLayout&lt;/code&gt; are deprecated. Outside a &lt;code&gt;StackLayout&lt;/code&gt; they act as if &lt;code&gt;AndExpand&lt;/code&gt; weren't there (&lt;code&gt;FillAndExpand&lt;/code&gt; behaves like &lt;code&gt;Fill&lt;/code&gt;). Replace them with a &lt;code&gt;Grid&lt;/code&gt;, or use the newer &lt;code&gt;HorizontalStackLayout&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;VerticalStackLayout&lt;/code&gt;, which behave like &lt;code&gt;StackLayout&lt;/code&gt; with &lt;code&gt;Orientation="Horizontal"&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;"Vertical"&lt;/code&gt;, with a few differences.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Even without those layouts, the app will look slightly different: margins, padding and the size of elements and text change. You can restore some of Xamarin.Forms' defaults as described in &lt;a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/maui/migration/layouts#default-layout-value-changes-from-xamarinforms" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Layout behavior changes from Xamarin.Forms&lt;/a&gt;, but expect to adjust screens by hand to make them look as they did.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;8. Replace custom renderers with handlers.&lt;/strong&gt; If a renderer derives from &lt;code&gt;FrameRenderer&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;ListViewRenderer&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;ShellRenderer&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;TableViewRenderer&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;VisualElementRenderer&lt;/code&gt;, you can derive it from the matching class in &lt;code&gt;Microsoft.Maui.Controls.Compatibility&lt;/code&gt; to get the app compiling quickly (&lt;a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/maui/migration/custom-renderers" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Reuse custom renderers&lt;/a&gt;). The long-term fix is to move each renderer to a handler (&lt;a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/maui/migration/renderer-to-handler" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Migrate a custom renderer to a handler&lt;/a&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;9. Find compatible packages.&lt;/strong&gt; Getting every library to work in MAUI is often the hardest part, and harder still when you upgrade package versions and the .NET version at the same time. It looks different for every app: some packages have MAUI versions, some have replacements, and for some you'll need to change the code or the source yourself. Our solutions for Firebase and Twilio Video are below.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;10. Test on real devices and publish.&lt;/strong&gt; Test on Android and iOS devices (not just emulators), fix layout and behavior differences, and check the app against your release checklist. Build with the SDKs the stores require today: Xcode 26 for the App Store, and Android 16 (API 36) as the target for Google Play.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Package migration: Firebase and Twilio Video
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These notes come from our migration of a home-care patient app that uses Firebase, and Twilio Video for its video visits.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Firebase
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You have two options:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Keep Microsoft's Firebase binding packages.&lt;/strong&gt; They're the same packages Xamarin used, so your project will still reference packages whose names start with &lt;code&gt;Xamarin.&lt;/code&gt; after the migration.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Switch to &lt;a href="https://github.com/TobiasBuchholz/Plugin.Firebase" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Plugin.Firebase&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;, a wrapper built for MAUI. It takes more code changes, but the &lt;a href="https://github.com/TobiasBuchholz/Plugin.Firebase/tree/development/docs" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;setup is well documented&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With either package, you may run into this build error: &lt;strong&gt;"Could not find a part of the path."&lt;/strong&gt; Some paths in the MAUI build are too long for Windows. In our case &lt;code&gt;Xamarin.Firebase.iOS.CloudMessaging&lt;/code&gt; failed to build. To fix it, enable long paths and move the NuGet package folder to a short path:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Close all Visual Studio instances.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Run PowerShell as Administrator and enable long paths:
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight powershell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;   &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;New-ItemProperty&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;-Path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"HKLM:\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\FileSystem"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;-Name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"LongPathsEnabled"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;-Value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;-PropertyType&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;DWORD&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;-Force&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Create a folder &lt;code&gt;C:\nuget&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;In PowerShell as Administrator, point NuGet at it:
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight powershell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;   &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Environment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;SetEnvironmentVariable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"NUGET_PACKAGES"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"C:\nuget"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Machine"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


  Twilio Video
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Twilio doesn't provide an official .NET MAUI library, but there are options:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Rebuild the video screens "the MAUI way"&lt;/strong&gt; with &lt;a href="https://github.com/the49ltd/The49.Twilio.Video" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The49.Twilio.Video&lt;/a&gt;, a community package.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Reuse your Xamarin custom renderers&lt;/strong&gt; with just the binding projects from that repository (&lt;code&gt;The49.Twilio.Video.iOS&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;The49.Twilio.Video.Android&lt;/code&gt;). You'll change some references and possibly some code to match Twilio Video API changes. When upgrading from an older Twilio version, replace &lt;code&gt;AddRenderer()&lt;/code&gt; with &lt;code&gt;AddSink()&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you reference the binding projects directly, Visual Studio may not recognize their namespaces. Reference the built DLL from the binding project instead.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Conclusion
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;.NET MAUI is the future of cross-platform app development with .NET. The tools help with the first pass, but plan for testing and manual fixes, especially for layouts, custom renderers and third-party packages. The app stores have now made the decision for you: without a migration, a Xamarin app can't be updated. Once you're on .NET MAUI, keep it current with a yearly upgrade.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  FAQ
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Is Xamarin still supported?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No. Microsoft ended support for all Xamarin SDKs, including Xamarin.Forms, on May 1, 2024. There are no more bug fixes, security updates or support for new Android and iOS versions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Can I still publish updates to a Xamarin app?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not to the app stores. Xamarin's last versions target Android API 34 and Xcode 15. Google Play now requires updates to target Android 16 (API 36), and the App Store requires builds made with Xcode 26.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Which .NET version should I migrate to?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;.NET 10, with .NET MAUI 10. .NET 8 and .NET 9 reach end of support on November 10, 2026, and MAUI 8 and 9 are already out of support.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Is there a tool that migrates a Xamarin app automatically?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not completely. The GitHub Copilot modernization agent in Visual Studio, which replaced the deprecated .NET Upgrade Assistant, handles much of the project and code conversion. Layouts, custom renderers and third-party packages still need manual work and testing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Do I need to rewrite my app to move to .NET MAUI?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Usually not. .NET MAUI is based on Xamarin.Forms, so most of your C# code and much of your XAML carries over. The work is in the project structure, layouts, renderers and packages.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;SharpQuest builds and migrates cross-platform mobile apps with .NET MAUI. More on that in our &lt;a href="https://www.sharpquest.com/mobile-app-development-company/?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;amp;utm_campaign=blog-xamarin-maui" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;mobile app development&lt;/a&gt; work.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;あなたの仕事と、大切なつながりを守るために — 第 5 回&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  何から始めればよいのだろう？
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ここまで、自分の仕事が多くの人やサービスにつながっていること、そして、そのどこかで問題が起きると、仕事そのものに影響することがあると考えてきました。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;では、何をすればよいのでしょうか。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;パソコンに詳しい人を雇う必要があるのでしょうか。高価なセキュリティ製品を導入しなければならないのでしょうか。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;そう考えると、少し身構えてしまうかもしれません。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;でも、最初からすべてを完璧にする必要はありません。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;大切なのは、 &lt;strong&gt;普段使っている道具やサービスを見直し、できることから少しずつ整えていくこと&lt;/strong&gt; です。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fch59b5iumd02b1ws0fg4.webp" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fch59b5iumd02b1ws0fg4.webp" alt="practical-security-checks" width="799" height="307"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  いつもの道具を、少しだけ見直してみる
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;仕事で使っているパソコンやスマートフォンを思い浮かべてみてください。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;毎日使っているからこそ、更新のお知らせを後回しにしたり、パスワードを使い回したり、以前に設定したファイルの共有状態をそのままにしたりすることがあります。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;どれも、特別なことではありません。忙しい毎日の中で、いつの間にかそうなってしまうことがあります。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;まずは、次のようなことを確認してみましょう。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;確認すること&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;できること&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;パソコンやスマートフォン&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;OS やソフトウェアを更新する&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;ウイルスなどへの備え&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;保護機能やウイルス対策ソフトが有効か確認する&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;パスワード&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;サービスごとに異なる、推測されにくいものを使う&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;ファイルやサービスの共有&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;必要な人だけが利用できる設定にする&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;大切なデータ&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;別の場所にもコピーを保管する&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;日々の情報収集&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;自分の仕事に関係する脅威や手口を知る&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;これは、IPA (情報処理推進機構) が中小企業や個人事業主向けに示している「情報セキュリティ 6 か条」の考え方にも沿ったものです。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;特別な設備がなくても、始められることはあります。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  特に大切なものは、なくさないようにしておく
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ここで、一つだけ少し詳しく考えてみたいことがあります。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;それは、仕事に必要なデータのバックアップです。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;顧客情報、注文記録、会計データ、仕事で作った書類。こうしたものが、パソコンの故障や誤操作などで失われてしまったら、仕事に大きな影響が出るかもしれません。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;そこで、必要なデータのコピーを、普段使っているパソコンとは別の場所にも保管しておきます。&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;    仕事用パソコン
          |
          v
      大切なデータ
          |
     定期的にコピー
          |
     +----+-----+
     |          |
     v          v
  外付け機器  別の場所・サービス
  (保管する)    (安全に保管)
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;ただし、コピーを作ればそれで終わり、というわけではありません。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;定期的にコピーされているか。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;元のパソコンと一緒に消えないようになっているか。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;必要なときに、きちんと元に戻せるか。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;こうしたことも、ときどき確認しておきましょう。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;例えば、外付けの記憶装置を使うなら、コピーを取った後にパソコンから取り外して保管する方法があります。クラウドサービスを利用する場合も、ファイルを同期するだけでなく、削除や書き換えが起きたときに以前の状態へ戻せるかを確認しておくと安心です。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;大切なのは、コピーを持つことだけではなく、困ったときにデータを取り戻せるようにしておくことです。&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F0x9wad4jugfteih3ial4.webp" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F0x9wad4jugfteih3ial4.webp" alt="data-backup" width="799" height="307"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  使っているサービスも、ときどき確認する
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;仕事で利用しているクラウドサービスや共有フォルダーも、一度設定したら終わりではありません。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;例えば、取引先と共有していたファイルが、必要なくなった後も閲覧できる状態になっているかもしれません。以前の担当者が使っていたアカウントが残っていることもあります。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;定期的に、次のような点を確認してみましょう。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;今も利用している人だけがアクセスできるか。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;必要のなくなったアカウントや共有設定が残っていないか。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;大切な情報を、誰でも見られる状態にしていないか。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;すべてを細かく調べる必要はありません。まずは、顧客情報や仕事の書類など、特に大切なものから確認してみるとよいでしょう。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  もし使えなくなったら、どうするだろう？
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ここまでは、普段使っている道具やデータを守るための話でした。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;もう一つ、事前に考えておくと役立つことがあります。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;それは、 &lt;strong&gt;仕事に欠かせないサービスが使えなくなったとき、代わりにどうするか&lt;/strong&gt; です。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;例えば、予約サービスが使えなくなったときに、電話で予約を確認できるでしょうか。メールが使えなくなったとき、取引先に連絡できる別の方法はあるでしょうか。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;完璧な代替手段を用意する必要はありません。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;重要な連絡先を、メール以外でも確認できるようにしておく。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;仕事を管理するための大切な情報を、必要な範囲で手元にも残しておく。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;困ったときに相談できる人や業者の連絡先を控えておく。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;こうした小さな準備が、いざというときの助けになります。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fexf4nrchodbjnoj7yzw4.webp" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fexf4nrchodbjnoj7yzw4.webp" alt="extra-alternative-work-methods" width="799" height="350"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  まずは、一つだけ始めてみる
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ここまで紹介したことを、もう一度整理してみましょう。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;OS やソフトウェアを更新する。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;ウイルス対策など、普段使う端末の保護を確認する。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;パスワードを見直す。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;ファイルやサービスの共有設定を確認する。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;大切なデータのバックアップを取る。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;脅威や攻撃の手口について知る。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;そして、仕事が止まったときに備えて、相談先や代わりの連絡方法を一つ考えておく。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;すべてを今日中に終わらせる必要はありません。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;例えば、今日はパソコンの更新を確認する。別の日に、大切なデータのコピーを用意する。時間があるときに、サービスの共有設定を見直す。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;そんな進め方で十分です。&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;     まず一つ選ぶ
          |
          v
      実際にやってみる
          |
          v
      続けられる形にする
          |
          v
      次の一つに取り組む
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;セキュリティ対策は、特別な技術を持つ人だけが行うものではありません。普段の仕事を少し見直すことから始められます。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;全部を守ろうとしなくても大丈夫です。まずは、自分の仕事にとって大切なものを一つ守ることから始めてみませんか。&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fvhb6nekmcv9zurmbl0sj.webp" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fvhb6nekmcv9zurmbl0sj.webp" alt="extra-everyday-team-security-habits" width="799" height="350"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;次回はいよいよ最終回です。もし実際に問題が起きてしまったら、何をすればよいのでしょうか。慌てずに相談し、仕事を取り戻していくための対応について、一緒に考えていきます。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


  Trace Shape Reveals Model Capability: 3B vs 7B
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Last week I traced the same agent running on two different model sizes. Same code, same task, same prompts. The only difference: the model.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Phone:&lt;/strong&gt; Qwen2.5-3B (3 billion parameters, running on a Pixel 8 Pro)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Legion:&lt;/strong&gt; Qwen2.5-7B (7 billion parameters, running on an RTX 4060)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I didn't read a single output. I just counted spans.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What the traces showed
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3B model:&lt;/strong&gt; Every trace was exactly 2 spans. One &lt;code&gt;agent.run&lt;/code&gt;, one &lt;code&gt;llm.call&lt;/code&gt;. Zero tool calls. Ever.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;7B model:&lt;/strong&gt; Mean 5.8 spans per trace. Multiple &lt;code&gt;llm.call&lt;/code&gt; spans, multiple &lt;code&gt;tool.call&lt;/code&gt; spans. It actually used tools.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The 3B model wasn't being lazy. It &lt;em&gt;couldn't&lt;/em&gt; do tool calls — not reliably, not in this setup. It would try to answer directly, skipping the tool-use loop entirely. The trace shape was degenerate: a straight line from start to finish, no branching, no tool interaction.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The 7B model, same code, same prompts, would explore: call a tool, read the result, call another tool, synthesize. The trace shape was rich — branching, iterative, agentic.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F0eu77pox8eg3mucnteuo.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F0eu77pox8eg3mucnteuo.png" alt="Trace shape comparison" width="800" height="396"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Model         Traces  Mean spans  Tool calls
------------  ------  ----------  ----------
Qwen2.5-3B    6       2.0         0 (degenerate)
Qwen2.5-7B    ~500    5.8         yes (agentic)
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


  Why this matters
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can tell how capable a model is by looking at the &lt;em&gt;shape&lt;/em&gt; of its traces, without ever reading the outputs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This isn't about benchmark scores. It's about behavioral fingerprinting. A 3B model and a 7B model don't just give different quality answers — they behave differently as agents. The 3B doesn't do the agentic loop. The 7B does.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you're evaluating models for agentic use, don't just look at accuracy. Look at trace shape:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Does it use tools, or try to answer directly?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;How many steps does it take?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Does the shape change with task complexity?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A model that can't produce rich trace shapes can't do agentic work, no matter what its benchmark scores say.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The pattern
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the second time trace shape has told the story:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Prime Agent experiment:&lt;/strong&gt; Same model, different environments → 3.8x shift in span counts. Environment is input.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;This experiment:&lt;/strong&gt; Same environment, different models → degenerate vs rich traces. Model size determines agentic capability.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In both cases, I never read a prompt or output. The metadata was enough.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's the thesis behind AgentPulse: &lt;strong&gt;trace shape is a behavioral fingerprint.&lt;/strong&gt; Whether you're detecting environment drift, model regressions, or capability gaps, the shape tells you first.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;All traces captured with AgentPulse (metadata-only). No prompts, outputs, or tool inputs were recorded.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


  Fixing a JSON‑Schema Break in the Content‑Automation Pipeline for Bluesky Posts
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TL;DR:&lt;/strong&gt; The automation stopped publishing to Bluesky because a stray comma broke the JSON schema used for post payloads. I updated the schema, patched the generation script, and added proper validation, getting the pipeline back online.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  The Problem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My daily content‑automation repo (&lt;code&gt;content-automation&lt;/code&gt;) generates Markdown for Medium, Substack, and JSON payloads for Bluesky. On 2026‑11‑02 the pipeline threw a validation error:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Error: data.posts[0].text should NOT have additional properties
    at Ajv.validate (node_modules/ajv/dist/ajv.js:123:15)
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The root cause was a typo in the generated &lt;code&gt;bluesky_es.json&lt;/code&gt; – an extra trailing comma after the last object made the file invalid JSON, causing the schema validator to reject the entire payload. The automation script silently skipped publishing, leaving Bluesky empty for the day.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  What I Tried First
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My first instinct was to manually edit the offending &lt;code&gt;bluesky_es.json&lt;/code&gt; in the repo and re‑run the pipeline:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;# Attempted quick fix&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;sed&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-i&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'s/,]$/]/'&lt;/span&gt; content/2026/11/02/content-automation/bluesky_es.json
npm run publish:bluesky
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The change removed the stray comma, and the script ran without throwing an exception, but the underlying issue remained: the generation step kept producing malformed JSON. Every new run would re‑introduce the error, so this “quick fix” was not sustainable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I also tried disabling schema validation in the &lt;code&gt;publish.js&lt;/code&gt; script:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// publish.js – temporary bypass&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// const valid = ajv.validate(schema, payload);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// if (!valid) { console.warn('Skipping validation'); }&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Skipping validation let the broken payload slip through, but Bluesky’s API rejected the request with a 400 Bad Request because the JSON payload still contained the stray comma. The API error looked like:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Invalid JSON payload: Unexpected token ',' at position 342
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Both approaches were dead ends because they treated the symptom rather than the cause.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  The Implementation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  1. Define a strict JSON schema
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I placed a schema file at &lt;code&gt;schemas/bluesky-post.schema.json&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"$id"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://vibecoding.com/schemas/bluesky-post.schema.json"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"array"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"items"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
    &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"object"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
    &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"required"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"text"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
    &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"properties"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
      &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"string"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"enum"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"progress"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"avance"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
      &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"text"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"string"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"minLength"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
    &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
    &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"additionalProperties"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
  &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This schema enforces that each post object contains only &lt;code&gt;type&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;text&lt;/code&gt;, disallowing stray commas or extra fields.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  2. Update the generation script
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;File: &lt;code&gt;scripts/generate-bluesky.js&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;require&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;require&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Ajv&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;require&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;ajv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ajv&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Ajv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;allErrors&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;schema&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;require&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;../schemas/bluesky-post.schema.json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;outputDir&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;__dirname&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;..&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;content&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;2026&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;11&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;02&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;content-automation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Helper to write JSON with deterministic formatting&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;writeJson&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;filePath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;writeFileSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;filePath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;utf8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Build payload&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;buildPayload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;lang&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;posts&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;lang&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;en&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;progress&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;avance&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;lang&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;en&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Finally pushed the English version of today’s Bluesky post into content/2026/11/01/content-automation/bluesky_en.json.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Finalmente resolví el bug que me impedía publicar en Bluesky: actualicé bluesky_es.json con el nuevo post de productividad.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;];&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;posts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Validate and write&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;en&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;es&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;].&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;forEach&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;lang&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;buildPayload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;lang&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;valid&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ajv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;validate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;schema&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;valid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Schema validation failed:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ajv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;errors&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;exit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fileName&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`bluesky_&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;lang&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;.json`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;writeJson&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;outputDir&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fileName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`✅ &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;fileName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt; generated`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Key changes:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Strict validation&lt;/strong&gt; – The script now aborts on any schema violation, preventing malformed files from being committed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Deterministic JSON&lt;/strong&gt; – &lt;code&gt;JSON.stringify&lt;/code&gt; with a 2‑space indent guarantees no stray commas.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Language‑aware payload&lt;/strong&gt; – The &lt;code&gt;type&lt;/code&gt; field now matches the language (&lt;code&gt;progress&lt;/code&gt; for English, &lt;code&gt;avance&lt;/code&gt; for Spanish), aligning with the schema’s &lt;code&gt;enum&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  3. Adjust the CI step
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;ci/publish-bluesky.yml&lt;/code&gt; now runs the generator before attempting to push:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight yaml"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;steps&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;Generate Bluesky payloads&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;npm run generate:bluesky&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;Publish to Bluesky&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;success()&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;npm run publish:bluesky&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If validation fails, the job stops, and the failure is visible in GitHub Actions, eliminating silent skips.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  4. Commit the new files
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The diff for the added JSON payloads (commit &lt;code&gt;8f74a93b&lt;/code&gt;) looks like:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight diff"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+[
+  {
+    "type": "progress",
+    "text": "Finally pushed the English version of today’s Bluesky post into content/2026/11/01/content-automation/bluesky_en.json. The diff shows the new payload."
+  }
+]
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight diff"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+[
+  {
+    "type": "avance",
+    "text": "Finalmente resolví el bug que me impedía publicar en Bluesky: actualicé bluesky_es.json con el nuevo post de productividad. El diff muestra el nuevo payload."
+  }
+]
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


  5. Update &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt; file tracks the state of each platform. The relevant change (commit &lt;code&gt;10cb2829&lt;/code&gt;) toggles the Bluesky flag:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight diff"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="gd"&gt;-  "bluesky_published": false,
-  "bluesky_uris": {},
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+  "bluesky_published": true,
+  "bluesky_uris": {
+    "en": "at://did:plc:.../app.bsky.feed.post/3kxyz",
+    "es": "at://did:plc:.../app.bsky.feed.post/3kabc"
+  },
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Now the metadata reflects successful publication, which downstream tools (e.g., changelog generators) can consume.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  Key Takeaway
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Never rely on “happy‑path” scripts for data that crosses service boundaries. Enforcing a JSON schema &lt;strong&gt;at generation time&lt;/strong&gt; catches structural bugs early, prevents silent failures, and makes CI failures explicit. A small schema file and a validation step add negligible overhead but dramatically improve reliability.&lt;/p&gt;




