&lt;h1&gt;


  「大きなブレークスルー」はソフトウェア側から来るのかもしれない
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Metaが四半期決算で「Questの販売台数は減少している」と認め、今後は“major breakthroughs（大きなブレークスルー）”を伴うVRヘッドセットだけを製品化すると明言しました。Reality LabsのQ3売上は4億4,300万ドル。AIグラスは伸びてはいるものの、VRの落ち込みを完全には埋めていません。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;このニュースを視覚トレーニングの現場から読み解くと、意味は少し違って見えます。プラットフォームが「次は何か」を問い直す局面は、汎用のヒット作ではなく、特定の課題に深く効くユースケースが評価される局面でもあります。ハードの新世代を待つより、既に手元にあるQuestの上で、どこまでソフトウェアが体験を変えられるか。エンジニアの視点では、そこが本当の設計課題です。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;弱視（amblyopia／lazy eye）や視覚的注意、両眼の協調に課題を抱える人にとって、VRは「面白いデバイス」ではなく「毎日続けられる訓練環境」になり得ます。問題は、訓練が退屈だと続かないこと。だからこそ、コンテンツ視聴という既存の行動をそのまま訓練に変換するアプローチが効いてきます。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Seven Sportsが開発したAmblyotubeは、Meta Quest向けのリクリエーション／教育ソフトウェアです。左右の目に異なる映像体験を提示し、YouTube形式のコンテンツをそのまま視覚トレーニングの場に変えます。設計のポイントは、特別なハードや接着パッチに頼らず、レンダリングパイプライン側で両眼に別々の刺激を与えること。アーキテクチャとしては、動画再生レイヤーの上に視覚刺激を重ねる構成だと考えると理解しやすいはずです。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;利き目側には調整可能なデジタルブラー（Dominant Eye Shader）&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;弱視側にはAI Sharpener（MFBF）が人物を検出し、赤い輪郭・黄緑のハイライト・一定リズムの明滅で注意を促す&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;任意でWedge Occlusionを使い、片眼ずつの視野を一部遮へいして両眼の綱引き（binocular rivalry）を軽減&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;マゼンタのフォーカスキューで両眼の融合（binocular fusion）を促し、立体感の処理を練習&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;LogMARの視力チェックや、利き目・重症度プリセット（Mild／Moderate／Severe）をアプリ内で選択&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;接着パッチや赤青メガネは不要。好きなコンテンツを見ながら、1日30〜40分を目安に続けられます。トレードオフを挙げるなら、専用ハードの没入感ではなく、日常の視聴行動に寄せることで継続性を取っている点。指標としても、派手なスペックより「毎日開けるか」を優先した設計だと言えます。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;「大きなブレークスルー」は、必ずしもハードウェア側からだけ来るとは限りません。既にあるデバイスを、誰かの毎日の課題に合わせて設計し直すことからも生まれます。次のヘッドセットを待つ間に、ソフトウェアで解ける問題は案外多く残っています。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;詳細・ダウンロードはこちら：&lt;a href="https://www.meta.com/en-gb/experiences/amblyotube/25906906972338493/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.meta.com/en-gb/experiences/amblyotube/25906906972338493/&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;※本アプリは視覚トレーニングを補助するツールであり、診断・治療・治癒を目的としたものではありません。推奨は1日30〜40分（最大1時間）、13歳以上。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;TL;DR: For a logistics password-reset flow, choose the template boundary that can be migrated and rolled back without changing recovery semantics. Keep the reset URL, expiry policy, and suppression decision under application control. A delivery-managed template is acceptable when it has a versioned contract, approval audit, and a way to render the final message before release. The deciding test is boring: can the team reproduce exactly why a message was sent, blocked, or made unusable?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the small choice matrix I use for that test:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Template boundary&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Migration shape&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Best fit&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Failure to plan for&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Application-owned&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Move the transport adapter behind one interface&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A security-sensitive reset flow with code review&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Copy changes wait for an application release&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Delivery-managed&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Move a template ID and variable contract&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;An editor needs independent publishing&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A published variable or link can drift from the caller&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Hybrid&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Move a constrained wrapper while keeping recovery fields in code&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Multiple regions or brands share one flow&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Two systems can claim authority over the final copy&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My default is application-owned copy. That is less about taste than rollback scope. If a depot operator cannot reset an account after a transport migration, the team needs one change set that can restore the previous wording, URL shape, and locale inputs. Keep the message portable first. Optimize editing rights second.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep the rollback boring.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How should a lightweight email API handle password reset emails?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The portable unit is not an HTML file. It is a small contract: recipient, reset URL, locale, and a server-defined expiry policy. The mail layer should not create recovery credentials, decide whether an account exists, or silently rewrite the URL. Those decisions belong to account recovery.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I use a 15-minute expiry in an example because the number is easy to inspect. It is not a universal policy. The server that validates the token and the message that displays its expiry must receive the same named policy value. A stale template that says “15 minutes” while the server uses another value is a user-visible defect even when the email API returns success.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Suppression is a separate eligibility input. A suppressed recipient should produce a durable blocked result, not an endless retry. An unavailable suppression lookup needs an explicit policy: bounded retry, or fail closed when the list is an authoritative privacy control. Do not turn a timeout into an accidental decision.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Email authentication has a different job. SPF describes which hosts are authorized to send for a domain; it does not tell the application whether one recipient should receive a password reset. SMTP status codes can help classify transport outcomes, but they do not replace the application’s recipient policy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Can a template move without breaking recovery?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Treat a template migration as a compatibility change. First publish version 2 with the same variable names and the same recovery action. Then render a representative message in CI. Finally, switch the template reference behind a configuration value that can be reverted independently of the account-recovery deployment. I initially treat a successful transport response as evidence that the migration worked; it isn't. The real check is the rendered recovery path: a dispatcher must be able to open the message, understand the short expiry, and reach the same recovery origin the application validates. If version 2 changes &lt;code&gt;resetUrl&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;token&lt;/code&gt;, the API call can still return success while the operator gets a dead flow. That is the kind of failure a screenshot review misses, and it is why the old version must remain selectable until the completed-reset metric is stable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The checks should look for behavior, not a pretty screenshot. Assert that the final message contains one reset action, the expected locale text, the intended expiry, and no token in the subject. Assert that the URL points to the recovery origin allowed by the application. Store the template version beside the delivery result so an operator can compare a failed reset with the exact published artifact.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is enough for a useful first gate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A useful fixture set is small: an active address, a suppressed address, a suppression lookup failure, and a changed template version. Four cases expose the boundary. A fifth can cover a malformed recipient if the application accepts untrusted input at that point. The test names matter more than a large pile of happy paths.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is where lightweight APIs often become heavy systems. The first call may be one function. The migration surface still includes credentials, DNS authentication, suppression state, message rendering, retries, and observability. I benchmark time-to-first-call, but I also count the glue that must survive the second transport. A low-friction integration that cannot export its template decision is not lightweight for long.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;DeliveryResult&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;accepted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;messageId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;templateVersion&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;suppressed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;reason&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;recipient-policy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;blocked&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;reason&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;suppression-unavailable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;failed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;retryable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;boolean&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;reason&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;PasswordResetMessage&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;accountId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;recipient&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;resetUrl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;expiresInSeconds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;locale&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;templateVersion&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kr"&gt;interface&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ResetDelivery&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;send&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;PasswordResetMessage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;DeliveryResult&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The interface deliberately exposes no vendor-shaped request object. The adapter can translate it to a chosen API, while the recovery service keeps one stable contract. The &lt;code&gt;templateVersion&lt;/code&gt; is data, not a comment in a deployment ticket. It makes rollback and incident review possible. It also gives a migration review a concrete boundary: version 1 and version 2 can be rendered against the same fixture without changing the recovery service.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Which ownership rules survive a transport change?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ownership has two parts that teams often mix together: who edits the words, and who owns the recovery meaning. Let an editor change a greeting or a depot-specific footer if the organization needs that freedom. Do not let an editable layer silently alter the reset action, destination origin, or displayed expiry unless the application can test the complete rendered result.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The contract should reject unknown variables. A caller sending &lt;code&gt;resetUrl&lt;/code&gt; should not pass because a template happened to expect &lt;code&gt;token&lt;/code&gt; last month. Pin a template version, allowlist its variables, and record the approval event. The error should be visible before delivery, not discovered by an operator clicking a dead message.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is a practical permission question too. Application-owned copy concentrates review in the code repository. Delivery-managed copy introduces another publishing surface and another rollback mechanism. Neither is automatically safer. The useful question is whether the team can identify the person, version, and test result associated with the change.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep logs useful and restrained. Record message type, template version, suppression decision, lookup timestamp, and a stable account identifier. Do not log the reset token or the complete rendered body. The latter can contain addresses, internal links, and localization data that do not belong in routine diagnostics.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  When is the runner-up the better choice?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Choose delivery-managed templates when non-developer editors genuinely need autonomous publishing and the organization can enforce versioned approvals, variable validation, and final-message rendering outside production. Without those controls, a migration can succeed at the transport layer while silently breaking the user workflow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Choose a hybrid boundary when several regions need different branding but one account-recovery service must remain authoritative. Keep the recovery action and expiry policy in the application data. Give the managed layer a constrained wrapper. If the final assembled message cannot be rendered in CI, the wrapper is too powerful.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The application-owned option remains the least complex default for a logistics operation with short-lived reset links and suppression requirements. It makes the recovery contract easy to move, test, and roll back. The trade-off is real: copy edits follow the application release process. That is a small cost compared with losing track of which system changed a security-sensitive message.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The decision is complete when a dry run can answer three questions: which template version was selected, why the recipient was allowed or blocked, and which recovery policy generated the link. If those answers require searching several dashboards and guessing at timestamps, the boundary is still too wide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Further reading
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7208" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;RFC 7208: Sender Policy Framework (SPF)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc5321" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;RFC 5321: Simple Mail Transfer Protocol&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc3463" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;RFC 3463: Enhanced Mail System Status Codes&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Forgot_Password_Cheat_Sheet.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;OWASP Forgot Password Cheat Sheet&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8058" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;RFC 8058: Signaling One-Click Functionality for List Email&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Models got dramatically better over the past year. But they got better for everyone. In that time, our team went from 5.3 to 13.7 PRs per person per month, and I personally went from 4.8 to 51.3. Did you improve that much too? If not, here are a few things that work for us and might well help you as well.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Same model, different results
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Claude, GPT, Gemini. We all have access to the same models. So when one team flies with a better model and another stays stuck in place, it's not the model. It's what the team built around it. Rules, tools, skills, CI.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To be honest right at the start: the models themselves did a big part of our speedup. I won't pretend we came up with everything ourselves. What I want to show is what we built around them and what I actually see in the numbers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;All of this is about the Keboola Connection repository. Five of us work on it in our team; other contributors come from other teams. I'm comparing the winter and spring at the turn of 2024 and 2025 with this summer and autumn.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Numbers first
&lt;/h2&gt;

