&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fgrgnra3nxsrgi5tsegti.webp" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fgrgnra3nxsrgi5tsegti.webp" alt="Weekly pull request counts" width="800" height="202"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Weekly PR counts in the repository from March to the end of October 2026.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Once again: part of this is purely better models. And most of our skills leave no trace, so take all the numbers below as a lower bound.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So what did we actually do?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Rules in one place
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We used to have only &lt;code&gt;CLAUDE.md&lt;/code&gt;. But we wanted to open the repo to other agents too, so in June 2025 we made &lt;code&gt;AGENTS.md&lt;/code&gt; the single entry point for rules. &lt;code&gt;CLAUDE.md&lt;/code&gt; now just imports it. And comments written by AI are signed. You can tell at a glance what a human wrote and what an agent wrote.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  One entry point for humans and agents
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In April 2026, Martin Zajíc, with whom I'm putting together our whole agentic setup, and I built &lt;code&gt;./bin/kbc&lt;/code&gt;. One CLI for everything. Running tests, static analysis, preparing the environment. Today it has 65 tasks, and 21 of our 33 skills refer to it. Plus &lt;code&gt;kbc doctor&lt;/code&gt;, which checks whether your environment is in order.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each of us uses it several times a day, and agents many times more. When the agent and the human use the same command, the agent doesn't have to guess how things are run here. And neither does the human :-)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Skills for repetitive work
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In March 2025, Martin wrote a guide for migrating endpoints. This February, he turned it into the &lt;code&gt;migrate-endpoint&lt;/code&gt; skill. In September, I followed up with the &lt;code&gt;migrate-highway&lt;/code&gt; skill.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then the migrations took off. We went from 3.7 migrated actions per month to 23.2. Of the 270 old Zend actions, 72 are left.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sure, the timing overlaps with the arrival of better models. But the main benefit of the skill is elsewhere. With one simple call, the agent knows what to do. You don't have to explain anything, show it anything or look after it. It does the whole thing by itself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The agent runs E2E tests by itself
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;run-e2e-ci&lt;/code&gt; skill triggers the E2E Single Suite workflow in CI. It can run a whole suite or just a single test. In October alone, we ran it 238 times.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;None of us has to deal with setting up a local environment anymore. And ours is really complicated because of testing Snowflake, BigQuery and other tricky cases. The author of a change just sets up CI, writes the test, and then anyone who changes the same code can run it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I wrote about how we sped up those E2E tests in the first place in &lt;a href="https://vojta.biberle.cz/en/blog/speeding-up-e2e-tests-in-github-actions" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;How I sped up E2E tests at Keboola&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Ready-for-review PRs without a human
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For me, this is probably the most important piece. The &lt;code&gt;babysit-pr&lt;/code&gt; skill watches over a PR. It handles failing CI and review comments. Agents talk to each other through GitHub comments and resolve most objections before a human even has to start paying attention.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So what comes out of the AI is a PR that's ready for review. Without any human involvement.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Seven to eight people use it, and in September and October the agent babysat over a hundred PRs a month this way.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A second review bot for when Copilot hits its limits
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;ai-review&lt;/code&gt; label adds our own review bot to a PR. It runs on Gemini and gives us another review instead of Copilot when Copilot runs out of limits. The code is on GitHub as &lt;a href="https://github.com/vojtabiberle/ai-codereviewer" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ai-codereviewer&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We've had it since March 2025, and in October alone others added it to PRs 183 times. Meanwhile, the median time to merge dropped from 49 h to 26 h, and that was even before the team started using Claude Code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  From Linear all the way to a PR ready for review
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;ship-task&lt;/code&gt; skill takes a task from Linear and drives it all the way to a PR ready for human review. 53 of my PRs were created this way, with a median time to merge of 15.9 h. For PRs of the same size without it, it's 49.2 h.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An agent can always handle a well-prepared task in Linear or an RFC, so of course preparation matters a lot. But again, the main advantage of &lt;code&gt;ship-task&lt;/code&gt; is that it makes our work easier. All it needs is a Linear issue ID or a markdown file with the task, and one simple prompt guides the agent through the whole journey. First, it analyzes the task again, because the code may have moved on since the task was written, and prepares a plan. That's the only point where it waits for my approval. Then it implements the task in an isolated worktree, does its own review, runs the tests including E2E, and opens a draft PR. Next, it starts &lt;code&gt;babysit-pr&lt;/code&gt; in a subagent, which watches CI and handles review threads from bots and signed AI reviews. It never resolves human threads by itself. When everything is green, it flips the PR to ready-for-review and keeps watching it until a human approves it. You write one quick line, approve the plan, and a few dozen minutes later you come back to a green PR that has already gone through the agentic review rounds and is waiting for human review.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The agent cleans up technical debt
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The PHPStan baseline. That file where you hide the errors you "don't have time for right now". We had 2,467 errors in it. Today there are 1,081. They're being cleaned up by a skill that picks an error, fixes it and verifies the fix.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is exactly the kind of work nobody ever has time for, and the agent doesn't mind.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Open doors for other agents
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The team mainly uses Claude Code. But we didn't want to lock ourselves into one agent. So we moved 31 skills to &lt;code&gt;.agents/skills&lt;/code&gt;, which means they also work with Codex and Copilot. Right after that, the first review comments from Codex showed up on PRs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The main benefit is elsewhere, though. Now people from other teams in the company can come with their own agents, and their PRs can mostly be merged without major changes. And we have the door open to other agents and experiments.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Where I'd start
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The overall PR volume grew mainly thanks to the models. The setup doesn't work miracles by itself. But without it, we practically couldn't handle that many PRs at all.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If I were building it again, I'd go in this order:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Rules in one place (&lt;code&gt;AGENTS.md&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;One CLI entry point for humans and agents.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Skills for repetitive work.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;CI the agent can run by itself.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;An agent that watches the PR until it's ready for review.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Independence from a single agent.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You get a better model for free. Everything around it, you have to build yourself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most American workers are employed "at will": either the worker or the employer can end the job at any time, for almost any lawful reason, with or without advance warning. So when a layoff notice arrives, a natural question follows: if my job was at-will anyway, was my employer still required to warn me?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In many cases, yes. At-will describes the general terms of the job. The WARN Act is a federal statute that requires advance notice of certain large layoffs and plant closings, regardless of whether the affected jobs were at-will. One does not cancel the other.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What at-will employment actually means
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At-will is the default rule in every US state except Montana. Under it, an employer can generally dismiss a worker without cause and without a notice period, and a worker can quit just as freely. Contracts and collective bargaining agreements can change that default for specific workers, but most workers have no contract guaranteeing a fixed term.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Importantly, at-will is not unlimited. An employer still cannot fire someone for an illegal reason, such as discrimination or retaliation for reporting wrongdoing. And at-will does not override other statutes that impose their own requirements — which is exactly where the WARN Act comes in.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What the WARN Act requires
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Worker Adjustment and Retraining Notification Act requires covered employers to give 60 days' written notice before a plant closing or mass layoff that meets its thresholds. In general, the law covers employers with 100 or more full-time employees, and it is triggered by a plant closing affecting 50 or more full-time workers, or a mass layoff affecting either 500 or more full-time workers, or 50 to 499 workers who make up at least one-third of the workforce at a site.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Notice must go to the affected workers (or their union representative), the state dislocated worker unit, and the chief elected official of the local government, and it must state whether the layoff is permanent or temporary and the expected dates.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;None of this depends on whether the jobs were at-will. A factory with 200 at-will employees that closes entirely still owes WARN notice if the thresholds are met. For these large events, the employer's usual freedom to end at-will jobs immediately is replaced by a statutory notice duty.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Where the two rules overlap — and where they do not
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The confusion is understandable: for most everyday terminations the at-will rule really does mean no notice is owed. If an employer fires one worker, or lays off a handful of people, WARN is not triggered at all. It only steps in when a layoff is large enough to cross its thresholds.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are also narrow exceptions that can shorten notice below 60 days: a faltering company seeking capital to stay open, unforeseeable business circumstances, and natural disasters. These excuse shorter notice; they do not come from at-will employment, and an employer cannot simply invoke "at-will" as an exception.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;State mini-WARN laws add another layer. Several states require longer notice or cover smaller employers than the federal law — New Jersey, for example, requires 90 days' notice and, in some cases, severance pay.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Can a worker sign away WARN rights?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is where at-will workers should be careful. A handbook statement that employment is at-will does not waive WARN rights. But a separation agreement signed at layoff time may include a release of claims, which can affect a worker's ability to pursue a WARN claim for back pay and benefits. Workers over 40 get special review protections for such releases: generally 21 days to consider an individual release (45 days for a group layoff) and 7 days to revoke after signing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The practical lesson is simple: an at-will label does not mean you were owed nothing, and a severance offer does not automatically mean you were owed nothing more. If a large layoff arrived with little or no notice, check whether WARN applied, how many workers were affected at your site, and whether the notice met the 60-day rule.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The numbers behind the question
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In the 90 days covered by Layoff Atlas as of November 4, 2026, the registry counted 687 notices covering 65,404 reported workers, with 26 counts unavailable. California led with 21,216 workers, followed by Washington with 17,320. The upcoming weeks show 3,224 workers effective the week of November 4, 6,224 the week of November 11, 5,939 the week of November 18, and 9,858 the week of November 25.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These are reported plans, not confirmed job losses, and coverage rules vary by state. But the volume shows WARN's notice rule is not a rare technicality: it touches tens of thousands of workers in any given quarter, most of them at-will.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What to do if you received little or no notice
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;First, keep the notice itself, or note the date you were told and the date your job ends. Second, ask how many workers at your site are affected, since WARN turns on site-level numbers. Third, check whether your state has a mini-WARN law with a longer notice period or a lower threshold. Fourth, do not rush to sign a severance release — use the review period the law gives you. Finally, file for unemployment promptly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At-will means an employer could end your job. It does not mean an employer could end a hundred jobs overnight without warning.&lt;/p&gt;

